<MethodSection id="scope-of-claim" title="What SPS measures and what it does not" class="sb-mkey">
  <div class="sb-prose">
    <p class="sb-ink">
      The Survey Parity Score measures <strong>distributional fidelity</strong>: how closely a synthetic
      respondent system's aggregate response distributions match the human response distributions
      recorded in these specific surveys: the Pew American Trends Panel (OpinionsQA), SubPOP,
      GlobalOpinionQA, and the General Social Survey. That is the entire claim. SPS does
      <strong>not</strong> measure the validity of synthetic respondents as substitutes for human research
      participants, the accuracy of any individual synthetic response, or the ability to predict
      real-world behavior, purchasing decisions, or future opinion. A high SPS means the system reproduces
      how surveyed populations answered these questionnaires. It does not mean the system understands
      people, or that its outputs generalize beyond the measured surveys' populations, topics, and time
      windows.
    </p>
    <p><strong class="sb-ink">If you cite SynthBench results, please include three disclosures with the number:</strong></p>
    <ol class="sb-mlist sb-mlist-num">
      <li>
        <strong class="sb-ink">Fidelity, not validity.</strong> Describe SPS as distributional fidelity to
        recorded survey responses, not as accuracy, correctness, or human-equivalence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="sb-ink">Dataset scope.</strong> Name the datasets the score was measured on (Pew ATP /
        SubPOP / GlobalOpinionQA / GSS). A score on these surveys is not a score on your survey.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="sb-ink">Operator disclosure.</strong> Note that the benchmark operator also ships a
        scored product, and link the <a href="#operator-disclosure">operator disclosure</a> so readers can
        weigh the conflict of interest themselves.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</MethodSection>
